  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,dx\\,dy=$",
  "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x]_{y-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}\u20610-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(y-1))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(y-1)-1)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(y-1)-1)\\,dy & =[\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(y-1)-y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)-1)-(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-1)-0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(1)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
